A||||17||Wannabe||3.4L DOHC Block||||1||
Z||000000||Wannabe||12-12-2000||03:09 PM||||The 3.4L DOHC engine uses essentially the same block as the rest of the 2.3/3.1/3.4 V6 family, except that is has been adapted for use with the DOHC heads. Does anyone know if it would be possible to run this block with pushrod heads? The main questions are, is the block machine to accept lifters and are the oiling systems compatible? Alternatively, does anyone know if there are anycomplications with fitting the crank, rods and pistons from the DOHC engine into a pushrod engine? The idea is that it is less expensive to use the DOHC bottom end, designed for 7000+ rpm, than to build up the regular pushrod bottom end to run at 7000+ rpm. Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||12-12-2000||03:54 PM||||Not without spending more for machining than a 3.4L engine complete would cost.<P>There are no provisions for lifters in the DOHC block. ||||reg||13
Z||000002||Will||12-12-2000||03:58 PM||||In that same vein, could plugs be machined to plug the lifter bores so that a pushrod 3.4 block could be used to build a TDC engine?<BR>     As I understand it, the crank, rods, pistons, etc. are dimensionally the same between the 3.4 pushrod and TDC engines, so that a TDC bottom end could be used in a pushrod block.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||12-12-2000||04:14 PM||||Boy, that's a good question. When we assembled the DOHC, I remember the previous lifter gallery had a cast-in passage to flow oil up to the heads. I think that might be difficult to fabricate. I have some pic's at home. I'll post some later of the lift area.<P>I think it's best to consider the blocks to be mutually exclusive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||12-12-2000||07:13 PM||||The crankshaft and connecting rods are the same for both motors. The pistons are different. I am using the four valve pistons in my 3.4-liter push rod v6.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-12-2000||08:52 PM||||Notice what's missing?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/r0300088_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaun41178||12-12-2000||09:04 PM||||Looking at that pic how does the upper valvetrain get oil?  What passages does the oil follow to lubricate the cam lobes?<P>I have an aluminum block and although it is machined with the lifter bores and return galleys for a pushrod engine I am wondering if it would be possible to use the DOHC heads on the aluminum block.  I guess what it boils down to is how the oil gets to the valvetrain in the DOHc setup and how the oil returns as well and how different that is from the pushrod block.<P>That plate on the lifter valley, at the far end in the pic there looks to be some kind of tube.  Is that where the oil flows up to the heads?  Is that block even machined for a camshaft?  If not then that idea probably won't work.  I wouldn't mind getting my hands on a DOHC block and compare to see if something could be worked out.  But by the pic it looks like they are totally different casting processes<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-12-2000||11:02 PM||||k <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dohc_oil.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||12-12-2000||11:54 PM||||Hmmm...no room there for lifters and pushrods. Nice pic btw. Sounds like it's still possible to use the dohc internals though. Seems kind of pointless though, I might as well just build up the regular 3.4 bottom end. Geez I wish I could just run the DOHC engine...life would be much simpler.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RedOktober||12-13-2000||12:04 AM||||Terry... is that oil cooler an add on or is it cast into the block?? <P>It would be interesting to see if it fits the std 2.8's.<P>thanks<P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||12-13-2000||01:24 AM||||The oil cooler is an adapter.||||reg||1
Z||000011||msweldon||12-13-2000||09:49 AM||||terryk,<P>What shop manuals did you use for the DOHC or do you recommend a certain one for doing a total tear-down and rebuild of the 3.4TDC block?<P>thanks,<BR>mw||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||12-13-2000||02:32 PM||||That's from the 93 factory shop manual. The only way to fly. Helms Inc. They are on the web. The problem is that the cars that use the DOHC all have three manuals. I don't think you can get them seperately. You might want to check on the 91 3.4L DOHC Supplement. It covers the engine, ECM, and trans, but might be cheaper.||||reg||1
Z||000013||msweldon||12-13-2000||04:48 PM||||hmmm...Is the supplement available from Helm.<P>I've got a 96' DOHC which I believe has a good number of mods in comparison to the 91-94 TDC's, TB, intake plennum/runners, and the exhaust manifold ports.  Looks like i'm going to have to suck it up and buy the whole helm shop set for the W-body's to get the 96' engine details.  <BR>Are these docs possibly available at the library?<P>thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||12-13-2000||05:26 PM||||A good library might. Mitchell's manuals.||||reg||1
